  "summary": "Researchers profiled over one million nuclei from human, macaque and mouse spinal cords using gene expression, chromatin and spatial methods. Cell identity and layout are highly conserved, and the dorsal horn differs most, including a primate-specific glycine receptor subunit switch. The atlas is a descriptive resource, and links to function and disease still need testing.",
